const content="<article data-editorial-version=\"3\" class=\"sn-page\"><div class=\"sn-read\"><a href=\"/supporters/\">← Featured sponsors</a><p class=\"sn-kicker\">Marine Diesel Engine · Sponsor feature</p><img class=\"sn-logo\" src=\"/images/supporters/boateye360/logo.webp\" alt=\"BoatEye360 logo\" width=\"210\" height=\"100\"><h1>BoatEye360: see other boats and objects in low light</h1><p class=\"sn-intro\">BoatEye360 supplies full-colour marine night-vision cameras for spotting vessels, markers and floating objects when light is limited. For owners who use their boats before dawn or after sunset, the product adds a live view of the surroundings at the helm.</p><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/boateye360/camera.webp\" alt=\"BoatEye360 C-1080 marine camera\" width=\"801\" height=\"801\"><section><h2>Visibility for the trips your boat makes</h2><p>A reliable engine helps the boat get where it is going; the operator still needs information about what is ahead. BoatEye360 uses low-light CMOS technology to create a colour picture from available light.</p><p>We like the clear purpose. It is a camera for seeing the actual scene, rather than another display of position or heading. Owners can assess that benefit according to how often their trips involve darkness or difficult low-light visibility.</p></section><section><h2>Connect the camera to a suitable screen</h2><p>The C-1080 has a 316L stainless-steel housing and HDMI and RCA outputs. Compatible marine displays can show the image, depending on their model and connections. A separate monitor is also available.</p><p>That gives owners different ways to plan an installation around an existing helm. We appreciate being able to consider where the image will be visible to the person operating the boat as part of the purchase.</p></section><section><h2>Why we feature BoatEye360</h2><p>Engine owners often look at the boat as a set of systems with specific jobs. BoatEye360 fits that approach: its job is improved low-light visibility, supported by marine camera hardware and display options.</p><p>For readers making regular early starts or evening returns, a live colour view is a practical capability to investigate. It adds information about nearby boats and objects during the trips the vessel is already being prepared to make.</p></section><div class=\"sn-contact\"><a href=\"https://boateye360.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Discover BoatEye360 cameras ↗</a><a href=\"/supporters/\">More featured sponsors</a></div></div></article><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"Article\", \"headline\": \"BoatEye360: see other boats and objects in low light\", \"description\": \"BoatEye360 supplies full-colour marine night-vision cameras for spotting vessels, markers and floating objects when light is limited.\", \"image\": \"https://marinedieselengine.com.au/images/supporters/boateye360/camera.webp\", \"author\": {\"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Marine Diesel Engine\"}, \"dateModified\": \"2026-11-05\", \"mainEntityOfPage\": \"https://marinedieselengine.com.au/supporters/boateye360/\"}</script>";
